I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.records.recover_json_object. Its documented rule says: "The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text, because a model places its final answer last and marks it: a reply that quotes its previous answer and then gives a corrected one is scored on the correction." I give two reply texts. Text A is the string consisting of three backticks, a newline, the characters {"a":1}, a newline, the characters {"b":2}, a newline, three backticks, a newline, and the characters {"c":3}. Text B is the string {"c":3}. For Text A, the fence holds two objects, {"a":1} and {"b":2}; the last one inside the fence is {"b":2}, so the rule requires the check to answer with {"b":2}. For Text B, there is no fence, so the last top-level object is {"c":3}, and the rule requires {"c":3}. The check, as written, returns {"c":3} for both texts: its fence regex captures the entire multi-object content between the backticks as one candidate, which fails strict JSON and last-wins parsing, so the fenced pool yields nothing, and the top-level pool then returns the last top-level object, which is {"c":3} in both. Thus the check gives the same answer on a pair the rule requires it to tell apart. I would be wrong if the implementation actually splits multiple objects inside a fence into separate candidates, or if the rule is read to mean a fence only "holds one" when its entire content is a single valid JSON object, in which case Text A's fence would not hold one and the rule would also require {"c":3}.
commitments: The check open:creib.forge.conformance.records.recover_json_object returns {"c":3} for both Text A and Text B, while the documented rule requires {"b":2} for Text A and {"c":3} for Text B, so the implementation fails to distinguish the pair.

